  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <p>Looking for a beach where your dog is welcome in Ramatuelle? Beyond Pampelonne, small wild coves welcome dogs on leash, year-round.</p>

      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Ramatuelle isn't just the large bay of Pampelonne. The coastal path that starts from Escalet beach heading towards Cap Taillat reveals several small, much wilder coves, such as the Douane cove, right by the customs officers' house built under Napoleon I. White sand, turquoise water and far fewer people than on the main beach.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The rule is the same as across the rest of the town: dogs on leash are allowed year-round, with no set hours — unlike neighbouring towns such as Rayol-Canadel-sur-Mer. These coves are not supervised and can only be reached on foot via the coastal path: there's no car park right nearby, so you need to park at Escalet or Pampelonne (Bonne Terrasse area) and walk.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: start from the Escalet car park (the furthest right one) and take the path to the right — the Douane cove is about a 10-minute walk away, well before Cap Taillat.</div>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>Are dogs allowed in Ramatuelle's coves?</h3>
      <p>Yes, on leash, year-round, with no set hours.</p>
      <h3>Is there a water point on site?</h3>
      <p>No, no water point is reported along the way — remember to bring water for your dog.</p>
      <h3>How do you reach the coves?</h3>
      <p>On foot only, via the coastal path, from the Escalet or Pampelonne car park.</p>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>For a more accessible, better-equipped beach, head to <a href="/en/fiche-pampelonne">Pampelonne Beach</a> or <a href="/en/fiche-bonne-terrasse">Bonne Terrasse Beach</a>. For a family-friendly beach with a dog shower, head to <a href="/en/fiche-escalet">Escalet Beach</a>, the starting point of the trail.</p>
    </div>

